{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the equation \\(10xy + 14x + 15y = 166\\) for positive integers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), we will follow a systematic approach.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in a more manageable form:\n\\[10xy + 14x + 15y = 166.\\]\n\nTo simplify, we can add and subtract 210 on the left side:\n\\[10xy + 14x + 15y + 21 - 21 = 166 \\implies 10xy + 14x + 15y + 21 = 187 \\implies (10x + 15)(y + 1) = 187.\\]\n\nNow, we need to factorize 187. The prime factorization of 187 is:\n\\[187 = 11 \\times 17.\\]\n\nThus, the possible factor pairs of 187 are \\((1, 187)\\), \\((11, 17)\\), \\((17, 11)\\), and \\((187, 1)\\). We will test each pair to find the corresponding values of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\n1. For the pair \\((10x + 15, y + 1) = (1, 187)\\):\n   \\[10x + 15 = 1 \\implies 10x = -14 \\implies x = -1.4,\\]\n   which is not a positive integer.\n\n2. For the pair \\((10x + 15, y + 1) = (11, 17)\\):\n   \\[10x + 15 = 11 \\implies 10x = -4 \\implies x = -0.4,\\]\n   which is not a positive integer.\n\n3. For the pair \\((10x + 15, y + 1) = (17, 11)\\):\n   \\[10x + 15 = 17 \\implies 10x = 2 \\implies x = 0.2,\\]\n   which is not a positive integer.\n\n4. For the pair \\((10x + 15, y + 1) = (187, 1)\\):\n   \\[10x + 15 = 187 \\implies 10x = 172 \\implies x = 17.2,\\]\n   which is not a positive integer.\n\n","prefix_end":1233,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,23606,17767,16,15,4130,488,220,16,19,87,488,220,16,20,88,284,220,16,21,21,57758,369,6785,25780,17767,87,57758,323,17767,88,59,701,582,686,1795,264,36438,5486,382,5338,11,582,18130,279,23606,304,264,803,70028,1352,510,78045,16,15,4130,488,220,16,19,87,488,220,16,20,88,284,220,16,21,21,7110,2533,1249,39721,11,582,646,912,323,32256,220,17,16,15,389,279,2115,3108,510,78045,16,15,4130,488,220,16,19,87,488,220,16,20,88,488,220,17,16,481,220,17,16,284,220,16,21,21